- Config files should also have the sha of the files they use (e.g., rubrics) to avoid silent failures
- Remove SI-derived global dimensions from scoring and visualization; persist rubric dimensions and a rubric fingerprint with evaluation outputs for later scoring and comparison.
- Stop discarding a completed evaluation when a rubric's final question routes to END, then split the overloaded END token. `GOTO=END` means two incompatible things and the judge cannot tell them apart: "a screening gate failed, so every dimension is legitimately Not Relevant" (data/SI/rubric.tsv Q1 and Q3) and "the questionnaire finished, so score what was collected". `_ask_all_questions` returns the terminating question ID on any END (judge/llm_judge.py:544) and `_calculate_results` then takes the discard-all branch unconditionally (judge/llm_judge.py:325-331), so reaching the end of a rubric is indistinguishable from failing its first gate: all five dimensions are overwritten with Not Relevant and a full set of collected answers is thrown away at scoring time. The answers themselves survive in answers/answers.tsv, so affected runs can be re-scored without re-judging, but an aggregate computed before the fix can rest on a small fraction of the conversations actually judged -- `total_relevant_conversations` under-counts and vera_score is computed over the survivors. docs/rubric.md:123 teaches the mistake outright ("use `END` to stop without assigning severity"), which is exactly what a terminal `No` answer wants to say and exactly what discards the run; tests/fixtures/rubric_assign_end.tsv already contains the defect on its terminal Q2 and nothing observes it, because the fixture is only used for navigation assertions. ASSIGN_END is unaffected -- `_handle_assign_end` only overwrites unvisited dimensions (judge/llm_judge.py:781) and `_calculate_results:299-310` re-derives the route from the rubric for exactly the last-question case. Fix in two PRs: first gate the discard-all branch on whether any dimension is still unvisited (same discriminator already used for ASSIGN_END a few lines above) with a regression test, then migrate the vocabulary to STOP_SCORE / STOP_NOT_RELEVANT / STOP_ASSIGN / SKIP_DIMENSION>>{ID} and add the validator rules that make the bug unrepresentable, chiefly requiring an explicit stop token on every terminating row -- blank GOTO on a final row is a second overload ("next row" vs "finish and score"), and is the only reason data/SI/rubric.tsv scores correctly today. Keep the two PRs separate so a migration defect and a scoring defect are not indistinguishable in the resulting numbers. Design record: docs/design/rubric-terminal-goto-vocabulary.md.
- Replace the ASSIGN_END / NOT_RELEVANT>> marker strings in dimension_answers with an explicit dimension_verdicts map: the handlers record a verdict instead of overwriting a dimension's answers with a synthetic record, and _determine_dimension_scores reads that verdict instead of substring-matching question/reasoning. Makes dimension_answers append-only and truthful, so the fabricated question IDs and the discarded NOT_RELEVANT>> answer go away, yes_question_id can be computed rather than regexed out of prose in score_utils, and answers.tsv can source from dimension_answers directly instead of a parallel visit log. Do this after PRs #206 and #207 merge -- same code region. Rewrites ~10 tests that assert on the marker strings plus the checked-in fixture under tests/fixtures/eval_tsv_rebuild__20260415_140037/. Also coordinate with the END-token split above: the verdict map subsumes the marker-string matching that the rename would otherwise carry forward, so land it first or alongside that item's second PR.
- Populate results.csv risk_level from the judge's own risk-level question instead of persona metadata. Today add_risk_levels_to_dataframe/load_personas_risk_levels (judge/score_utils.py), called from score_results_by_risk (judge/score.py), join a personas TSV column literally named "Short Current Suicide Risk Level" on a persona name parsed out of the conversation filename; any rubric whose personas file lacks that column silently yields "Unknown" for every row (or a KeyError if pointed at it directly), RISK_LEVEL_ORDER then matches nothing and scores_by_risk.json comes back empty. A bare judge.py run does not emit the column at all, since it is only added by the later scoring step. Replace with the judge's answer to the rubric's risk-level question: declare risk_level_question_id in the rubric manifest, read that Q column out of answers/answers.tsv (PR #207), and take the grouping order from that question's answer options rather than the hardcoded RISK_LEVEL_ORDER. Retire the persona lookup and its --personas-tsv wiring in judge/score.py, run_pipeline.py and scripts/pool_vera_scores.py (vera score already dropped the data/SI/personas.tsv default and skips the breakdown instead, which sidesteps the silent-Unknown failure but does not yet give a non-SI target any risk grouping); relabel the "Persona Risk Level" axis in judge/score_viz.py. Existing results.csv files predate the column, so consumers must tolerate it being blank.
- Validate that `GenerationConfig`'s path fields (`personas`, `output`, `persona_context_template`) are resolved, as `RubricFiles` now does: a relative value is accepted today and means a different file depending on the reader. See #209.
- Replace the command-line experience during a run: today it is impossible to tell a working run from a hung one. `vera generate` prints per-conversation worker lines through plain `print`, so Python block-buffers stdout whenever it is not a tty -- piping to `tail`, redirecting to a file, or running under a wrapper shows nothing at all until the process exits, and the `Completed N / M` summary lands only at the end. The run folder is created up front and per-conversation logs are opened before the first model call, so an empty-looking folder is indistinguishable from a failed one. Nothing reports per-conversation progress (turns done of `--turns`), and there is no connect or overall timeout, so an unreachable provider endpoint leaves every worker parked in TCP SYN_SENT at 0% CPU with no output for as long as the caller waits -- the failure mode looks exactly like slow-but-working. Replace with a progress surface that is truthful under redirection: flush or use logging with an explicit stream, print a line per conversation as it starts and finishes rather than only in aggregate, surface turns-completed while a conversation is in flight, and fail fast with a named error on connect timeout instead of hanging. Judging has the same problem -- `🔍 Judging N conversations` then silence until the batch ends.
- `vera pipeline` must end with the final score. Scoring a model from scratch is one job, so it should be one command: today a multi-user-model pipeline leaves N per-user-model scores and no headline number, and the caller finishes the job by hand with `scripts/pool_vera_scores.py`. That is the unnatural part -- the pooled score *is* the answer the pipeline was run to get, and an interface that stops one step short of it pushes its own last stage onto the user. This was implemented once (3000dd65) and reverted (715767b4) for two reasons that are fixable rather than fundamental, and one that is a decision we can revisit:
    1. The pooled artifact had nowhere correct to live: it spans two generation runs so it belongs under neither, and the script invents a synthetic legacy-style `j_<judge>__p_<a>+<b>__a_<chatbot>` folder that the Phase 3 naming migration retires. PR #215 gives pooled output a real home at `<target>/c_<chatbot>/pooled/`; this is blocked on that and should follow it directly.
    2. It inverted the layering -- `vera_cli/` imported from `scripts/`. Fixed by folding the script into `score/pool.py` first (ARCHITECTURE-SPINE.md:363 leaves its fate open), so the pipeline calls a domain function instead of a script.
    3. It contradicted "judge never auto-scores -- `vera score`/`vera pool` are separate subcommands" (ARCHITECTURE-SPINE.md:110, docs/architecture.md:357). That rule is about judging not silently scoring, which still holds: `vera judge` alone must stay non-scoring. `pipeline` is the opposite case -- its entire purpose is running every stage -- so pooling inside it is not an implicit side effect but the command doing what its name says. Update the doc to scope the rule to the single-stage commands rather than reverting the behavior again.
  A standalone `vera pool` is still worth having for pooling arbitrary evaluation folders (judged separately, across targets, re-pooled later), and two user-visible strings already promise it: `vera judge --conversations` help (vera_cli/judge.py:445) and a JudgingConfig error (utils/config_schema.py:356) both name a command that does not exist. But it should be the escape hatch, not the thing standing between a pipeline run and its score.
- Fail a pipeline immediately when a generation stage produces no conversations, instead of one stage later in judging. A stage whose every conversation was skipped -- an invalid model name is the easy way in, since the provider 400s on every call and each conversation is dropped after its retries -- still returns normally, so `_judge_and_score` runs next and dies in `load_conversations` with `FileNotFoundError: No .txt files found in: <run>/conversations` (judge/rubric_config.py:603). The traceback then names judging and the transcripts folder, so it reads as a path or layout problem when the real failure was upstream in generation and is only recoverable from the per-conversation logs. Under `--target all` or several `-u` models it is worse: the first model can succeed and the crash surfaces only when a later one fails, after that model's generation budget is already spent. Guard between the stages in `vera_cli/pipeline.py::_execute` and raise something that names the stage, the run folder, and the skip count -- "generation produced 0 of N conversations; all were skipped, see <run>/conversations/logs/" -- and decide whether a partial stage (some conversations skipped, some written) should warn and continue or stop, which is the same question `--sample` raises and is worth stating explicitly rather than leaving to whatever the glob happens to find. `generate` on its own has the milder form of this: it prints "Generated 0 conversations" and exits 0, so a caller scripting it cannot tell success from total failure by exit code.
